Texy! is sexy!
**************

<div class="perex">
Looking for a tool that turns Markdown and other human-written text into clean, good-looking HTML?

- Typesets quotes, dashes and non-breaking spaces for you
- Always produces well-formed HTML, even from broken input
- Safely handles comments and posts from visitors
- Reads Markdown and also offers the richer Texy syntax
</div>


Installation
============

Download and install the library using [Composer |https://getcomposer.org]:

```shell
composer require texy/markdown
```

This gives you Texy together with Markdown support. If you only want to write in Texy syntax, `composer require texy/texy` is enough. Texy 4.0 requires PHP 8.4 or later.


The First Article
=================

Imagine you have a blog and write your articles in Markdown. The beginning of one of them might look like this:

```
# How We Dried Plums

This year we picked **over 40 kg** of plums. What to do with them? Grandma says: "Dry them, and do it properly."

We bought the dryer at the [shop by the station](https://example.com/dryer) for $60-80.
```

How much code does it take to turn this into HTML? Surprisingly little:

```php
require __DIR__ . '/vendor/autoload.php';

$markdown = new Texy\Markdown\Markdown;
$markdown->config->locale = 'en';         // the article is in English
$html = $markdown->toHtml($text);         // $text holds the article above
```

The `locale` line tells Texy which language the article is written in, so it can pick the matching quotes. English is the default for Markdown, so here you could leave the line out; with `'cs'` you would get Czech quotes instead. And this is the result:

```html
<h1>How We Dried Plums</h1>

<p>This year we picked <strong>over 40&nbsp;kg</strong> of plums. What to do with
them? Grandma says: “Dry them, and do it properly.”</p>

<p>We bought the dryer at the <a href="https://example.com/dryer">shop by the
station</a> for $60–80.</p>
```

The heading, the bold text and the link are exactly what you expect from Markdown. But notice what you never typed: the quotes are curly, the price range has an en dash instead of a hyphen, and a non-breaking space keeps the number together with its unit. We show it here as `&nbsp;` to make it visible; the real output contains the character itself.

Texy reads Markdown as defined by the CommonMark specification, along with the GitHub Flavored Markdown extensions such as tables, task lists and strikethrough. You turn them on by creating the object with `Texy\Markdown\Markdown::gfm()` instead of `new Texy\Markdown\Markdown`. Texy passes all 652 examples of the CommonMark specification and all 24 examples of the GFM extensions. Everything you can write in Markdown is listed on the [Markdown Syntax |markdown] page.


Typography
==========

Texy polishes your text the way a typesetter would, and all you type are the characters on your keyboard:

- it replaces straight quotes `"…"` and `'…'` with the quotes used in the text's language: in English “…” and ‘…’, in Czech „…“ and ‚…‘
- it replaces a hyphen between numbers `9-17` with an en dash `9–17`
- it replaces three dots `...` with an ellipsis `…`
- it puts a non-breaking space between a number and its unit, and in Czech, Slovak and Polish also after one-letter prepositions and conjunctions such as `k`, `s`, `v` or `z`, so that a line never breaks between them and the word they belong to
- it hyphenates overly long Czech and Slovak words by syllables, so that narrow columns aren't left full of gaps

Texy knows the quotation marks of 18 languages, and you set the language with a single line, as the first article showed. Hyphenation of long words is off by default for Markdown, and turning it on is just as easy:

```php
$markdown->config->hyphenation = true;
```

Other replacements, such as the arrow `-->`, the `×` sign in dimensions like `10x20`, or `(C)`, are described in [Texy Syntax |syntax#Typography]. They apply to Markdown just the same.


Broken HTML
===========

Markdown lets you write HTML right in the text. What if the author forgets to close a tag, or closes tags in the wrong order?

```
Grandma <b>surely <i>loved it</b>.
```

Texy fixes it according to the rules of HTML:

```html
<p>Grandma <b>surely <i>loved it</i></b><i>.</i></p>
```

It always does this, so a single unclosed tag can never break the rest of the page.


Visitor Comments
================

You want comments under your articles. Anyone can post there, so you set up a separate object for them, in safe mode. Separate, so that the strict rules for comments don't apply to your own articles:

```php
$comments = new Texy\Markdown\Markdown;
$comments->config->locale = 'en';
Texy\Markdown\Configurator::safeMode($comments);
$html = $comments->toHtml($comment);    // $comment is the text of the comment
```

Let's see what it does with a comment whose author tries to sneak in a script:

```
Great recipe! <script>alert(1)</script> Check out [my website](https://example.org).
```

```html
<p>Great recipe! &lt;script&gt;alert(1)&lt;/script&gt; Check out <a
href="https://example.org" rel="nofollow">my website</a>.</p>
```

The script won't run, but it doesn't vanish without a trace either: readers see exactly what the author wrote, just as harmless text. The link stays and gets `rel="nofollow"`, so search engines won't treat spam in your comments as your endorsement. Safe mode never creates links to dangerous URLs, such as `javascript:`, in the first place.

What exactly safe mode does and how to fine-tune it is described on the [Configuration |configuration#Safe Mode] page.


Custom Extensions
=================

Want to embed a YouTube video in an article? You can teach Texy new things without touching its code. Just tell it how to render an image whose URL starts with `youtube:`:

```php
use Texy\Nodes\ImageNode;
use Texy\Output\Html;

$markdown->htmlOutput->addRenderer(function (ImageNode $node, Html\Renderer $renderer, ?Closure $previous) {
	if (!str_starts_with($node->url ?? '', 'youtube:')) {
		return null; // Texy renders other images as usual
	}
	return new Html\Element('iframe', [
		'src' => 'https://www.youtube.com/embed/' . substr($node->url, 8),
		'width' => $node->width ?: 640,
		'height' => $node->height ?: 360,
		'allowfullscreen' => true,
	]);
});
```

Texy calls the function for every image in the text. If the image's URL doesn't start with `youtube:`, the function returns `null` and the image is rendered the usual way. Otherwise it returns an `Html\Element`, an HTML tag that Texy outputs in place of the image. We don't need the `$renderer` and `$previous` parameters here; you'll find out what they are for on the page linked below.

The author of the article then simply writes `![Drying video](youtube:dQw4w9WgXcQ)`. How to change the rendering of any element is described on the [Customizing Elements |custom-handlers] page.

You can even extend Markdown with a completely new syntax, just as GitHub extended it with tables and task lists. For example, `@@jane` in a comment could become a link to that user's profile, as the [Adding Custom Syntax |custom-syntax] page shows.


When Markdown Isn't Enough
==========================

An image with dimensions and a description, aligned right, with a CSS class and a caption below it? In Markdown you would have to write HTML. That's why Texy also has its own syntax, which at its core is as simple as Markdown but is built for things like this. In Texy syntax, that image takes a single line:

```texy
[* dryer.jpg 300x200 .(Dryer in the attic)[photo] >] *** Photo: Jane Smith
```

Text in Texy syntax is converted by the `Texy\Texy` object, the engine Markdown itself is built on. No other package is needed. Unlike Markdown, it defaults to Czech, so for English text you would add `$texy->config->locale = 'en'` as in the first article; this example comes out the same either way:

```php
$texy = new Texy\Texy;
$html = $texy->toHtml($text);
```

```html
<figure style="float:right"><img src="dryer.jpg" alt="Dryer in the attic"
class="photo" width="300" height="200">
	<figcaption>Photo: Jane Smith</figcaption>
</figure>
```

`300x200` are the dimensions, the text in parentheses describes the image for those who can't see it, `[photo]` adds a class, `>` aligns it right, and `***` is followed by the visible caption. The part starting with the dot is called a modifier, and you can attach it the same way to a paragraph, a heading, a link or a table cell.

Texy syntax also handles tables with merged cells, table captions, and headings that balance themselves: whichever underline the author starts the article with, its most important heading gets the level you set in the [configuration |configuration#Headings]. You'll find everything on the [Texy Syntax |syntax] page.


Source Text Tools
=================

Your blog has moved to HTTPS, but dozens of articles stored as `.md` files still link to `http://my-blog.com`. Searching and replacing across files is a gamble: you'll also replace URLs in code samples that you want to leave alone.

Texy gives you a document tree in which every link knows precisely where its URL sits in the source text. You change just the URL, and the rest of the text stays exactly as it was:

```php
$source = Texy\Markdown\Markdown::normalize(file_get_contents($file));
$document = $markdown->parse($source);
$rewriter = new Texy\Rewriter($source);

foreach ($document->find(Texy\Nodes\LinkNode::class) as $link) {
	if ($link->urlRange === null) {
		continue; // a link whose URL is not written in the source text
	}
	$url = substr($source, $link->urlRange->offset, $link->urlRange->length);
	if (str_starts_with($url, 'http://my-blog.com')) {
		$rewriter->replace($link->urlRange, 'https://' . substr($url, 7));
	}
}

file_put_contents($file, $rewriter->getResult());
```

Here we call `parse()` instead of `toHtml()`; it only parses the text into a tree and produces no HTML. Before that, `normalize()` unifies line endings and similar details so that the positions in the tree match the text exactly. A URL in a code sample isn't a link, so it stays as it was. The one exception is link definitions, the `[name]: http://…` lines: their URLs aren't in the tree, so you have to handle them separately. Files in Texy syntax work the same way, just with `Texy\Texy::normalize()` and `$texy->parse()`.

The same foundation lets you build bulk rewrites, editor support, or a documentation checker that reports errors with line numbers. And because both languages share one tree, you can convert an article from Markdown to Texy syntax with a single call to `$markdown->toTexy($text)`. The [Source Text Tools |source-tools] page shows how.


In Latte Templates
==================

Do you write templates in [Latte |https://latte.nette.org]? Texy provides a filter for them, so you can convert an article right in the template:

```latte
{$article->text|texy}
```

How to register the filter and how to convert Markdown with it is described on the [Texy in Latte |latte] page.


Written in Texy
===============

This manual is written in Texy syntax, and so is the Nette documentation at [nette.org |https://nette.org]. The source of every page is on [GitHub |https://github.com/dg/deegee-docs], so you can see what any page looks like before it is converted to HTML.


History
=======

Texy was created by David Grudl in 2004 as one of the first text formatters for PHP. It originally ran on PHP 4, has gone through many versions since, and today takes advantage of what PHP 8 offers.

Version 4.0 turned it into an engine: the text is first parsed into a document tree, and only that tree is turned into HTML, plain text, or back into source text. Markdown and Texy syntax are built on the same core, so they get the same typography, the same HTML fixes and the same tools. How it works inside is described on the [Architecture and Principles |architecture] page.

